<Base title="The Waitlist — Basit Masood" description="Sixteen Pakistani fintechs got an EMI license. Two reached commercial scale. SadaPay was one of them. A three-stage growth system designed where capital didn't work.">

<script is:inline>
  (function(){
    var apply = function(){ document.body && document.body.classList.add('page-light'); };
    var clear = function(){ document.body && document.body.classList.remove('page-light'); };
    apply();
    document.addEventListener('astro:page-load', apply);
    document.addEventListener('astro:after-swap', apply);
    document.addEventListener('astro:before-swap', clear);
  })();
</script>

<Loader />
<Cursor />
<Nav active="stories" />
<FsMenu />

<!-- HERO -->
<section class="sp2-hero">
  <div class="sp2-hero-inner">
    <p class="sp2-hero-eyebrow">
      <a href="/stories/sadapay" style="color:inherit;text-decoration:none;">← SadaPay</a> · Case study · 2020 – 2024
    </p>
    <h1 class="sp2-hero-primary">I didn't design a waitlist.</h1>
    <p class="sp2-hero-secondary">I designed a three-stage growth system.</p>
    <p class="sp2-hero-desc">How I designed Pakistan's first gamified fintech waitlist into a three-stage growth machine. A behavioural design system built on user psychology, gamification mechanics, and habit-loop principles — not paid acquisition.</p>
  </div>
  <span class="sp2-hero-scroll">Scroll</span>
</section>

<!-- BRAND ANCHOR -->
<section class="sb-brand">
  <div class="sb-brand-inner">
    <img src="/images/Sadapay/Full-logo.svg" alt="SadaPay" class="sb-brand-logo sb-brand-logo--large" />
    <div class="sb-brand-rule"></div>
    <p class="sb-brand-caption">SadaPay · Waitlist case study · 2020 – 2024</p>
    <p class="sb-brand-role">Role · Founding Designer → Head of Product Design · Team 1 → 8</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- THESIS PULL-QUOTE -->
<section class="sub-pullquote sub-pullquote--wide">
  <div class="sub-pullquote-mark">&#8220;</div>
  <p class="sub-pullquote-text">The first Pakistani fintech to generate genuine FOMO.</p>
  <p class="sub-pullquote-cite">SadaPay · pre-launch, 2020</p>
</section>

<!-- DESIGN PHILOSOPHY — establishes the thesis up top -->
<section class="sub-section sub-section--editorial">
  <div class="sub-section-inner">
    <span class="sub-section-label">Philosophy</span>
    <div>
      <h2 class="sub-section-heading">Designing for the<br/><span class="accent">next user</span>, not the current one.</h2>
      <div class="sub-section-body">
        <p>Pakistani banking conventions had been written for users who grew up with branches, paper forms, and tellers. I wasn't designing for them. I was designing for the next generation of Pakistanis — the ones entering the financial system for the first time. Simplicity, intuitiveness, and the flexibility to scale into that audience as it grew up.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="sub-pullquote sub-pullquote--wide">
  <div class="sub-pullquote-mark">&#8220;</div>
  <p class="sub-pullquote-text">SadaPay deliberately designs for those who are young and yet to enter the market.</p>
  <p class="sub-pullquote-cite">Design philosophy, on the record · Bootcamp 2024</p>
</section>

<!-- EMI LANDSCAPE VISUAL — market backdrop -->
<figure class="wl-landscape">
  <img src="/images/Sadapay/pakistan-emi-landscape.png" alt="Pakistan's EMI Landscape table showing the EMI players in Pakistan at the time of SadaPay's growth phase, with their products, status, and headquarters." class="wl-landscape-img" loading="lazy" />
  <figcaption class="wl-landscape-caption">Pakistan's EMI landscape during SadaPay's growth phase. The competitive backdrop the design had to cut through.</figcaption>
</figure>

<!-- CHALLENGE -->
<section class="sub-section sub-section--editorial">
  <div class="sub-section-inner">
    <span class="sub-section-label">Challenge</span>
    <div>
      <h2 class="sub-section-heading">Capture the market<br/>by <span class="accent">resetting banking</span>.</h2>
      <div class="sub-section-body">
        <p>Pakistan had banks — bureaucratic, paper-first, designed for the teller, not the user. SadaPay wasn't building another bank. We were resetting what banking should feel like in a country that had never seen one done right.</p>
        <p>December 2020. No app, no card, no users. A pilot license capped at 1,000 onboardings. The brief I held with the CEO was contrarian: design demand, don't buy it. Build hype as a product, not as a marketing line.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- HIGHLIGHT -->
<section class="sub-highlight">
  <span class="sub-highlight-value">150,000+</span>
  <span class="sub-highlight-label">Pre-launch signups before we shipped a single transaction · Mastercard newsroom, Feb 2021</span>
</section>

<!-- APPROACH -->
<section class="sub-section sub-section--editorial">
  <div class="sub-section-inner">
    <span class="sub-section-label">Approach</span>
    <div>
      <h2 class="sub-section-heading">Three reward units.<br/>Three lifecycle stages.</h2>
      <div class="sub-section-body">
        <p>Pre-launch growth and post-launch growth need different mechanics. I designed three systems, each calibrated to where the product was in its journey.</p>
        <p><strong>Stage 1, pre-launch</strong> — status. <strong>Stage 2, bridge</strong> — scarcity. <strong>Stage 3, post-launch</strong> — cash.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- STAGE ONE -->
<section class="sub-section sub-section--editorial">
  <div class="sub-section-inner">
    <span class="sub-section-label">Stage one — Pre-launch</span>
    <div>
      <h2 class="sub-section-heading"><span class="accent">Exclusivity</span><br/>as mechanic.</h2>
      <div class="sub-section-body">
        <p>I designed the waitlist as a behavioural system, not a holding pen. The thesis: if waiting itself felt like the product, users wouldn't drop off. So I stacked three psychology principles into a single screen.</p>
        <p><strong>Loss aversion</strong> — the queue position, typeset huge. Every visit reminded users what they'd lose if they didn't climb. <strong>Variable rewards</strong> — golden tickets dropped randomly, skip-the-line tiers paid out asymmetric jumps (1 friend = 23,994 places, 3 = 32,000, 7 = 38,000 + the Founders Club black card). Slot-machine cadence inside a queue. <strong>Social proof and the IKEA effect</strong> — a public leaderboard turned invites into status, and the effort of climbing made users protective of the place they'd built.</p>
        <p>The output wasn't a waitlist. It was a daily habit loop. Users opened the app to check their position, invite another friend, see who'd overtaken them overnight. Waiting became the engagement.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ONBOARDING FLOW STRIP — the funnel users went through before seeing the queue -->
<figure class="wl-shot wl-shot--strip">
  <img src="/images/Sadapay/onboarding-flow-strip.png" alt="The full 11-screen SadaPay pre-launch onboarding funnel: Splash, Phone number, Verification code, Set PIN illustration, PIN entry, Confirm PIN, Personal info, Personal info confirm, Are you a freelancer SadaBiz upsell, Waitlist landing, and Waitlist scrolled state." class="wl-shot-img" loading="lazy" />
  <figcaption class="wl-shot-caption">The pre-launch funnel. Phone, PIN, CNIC, and a SadaBiz branch — all completed before the user saw their queue position.</figcaption>
</figure>

<!-- HIGHLIGHT: 1,566 invites stat from production leaderboard -->
<section class="sub-highlight">
